## Progress

<!-- /implement updates these as it works. Do not edit manually. -->
- [x] AC #1 — `github-project-routing.test.ts`: linked PR → linked project (not primary), unlinked → primary (siblings deliberately ordered so a lingering first-match answers wrong), unshared repo → unchanged, stale link → primary + warn, check-suite uses the nested PR number.
- [x] AC #2 — `projects-repo-validation.test.ts`: duplicate repo rejected naming the owner via pre-check, and the 23505 race maps to the same BAD_REQUEST rather than a 500.
- [x] AC #3 — same file: explicit secondary accepted, second primary rejected naming the incumbent, demoting the last primary rejected, self re-save allowed.
- [x] AC #4 — 26 new tests: 23 unit (9 routing + 14 validation) + 3 integration appended to `tests/integration/db/projects-repo-topology.test.ts`.

**Review — four rounds, seven must-fixes. Converged at round 4 with zero code findings.**

**Round 1 — three must-fixes. Each had ticked an AC that was not met.**
1. **The 23505 race path never fired.** drizzle wraps the pg error, so `code`/`constraint` sit on `.cause` — `src/api/routers/users.ts` already documents this and walks the chain, and plan 1's own integration test asserts `{ cause: { code: '23505' } }` against this very index. My unit test passed only because it fabricated the flat shape it wanted; in production the operator got the generic 500 AC #2 forbids. Test now uses the shape drizzle actually produces.
2. **Link-first routing was decision-only.** `dispatchWithCredentials` ignored the project the pipeline had just resolved and re-resolved by repo — first match — so on a shared repository the agent would run with ANOTHER project's GitHub token, PM provider and trigger context while the work-item lock was held under the linked one. Mutation-proved.
3. **Zero primaries was saveable.** The no-siblings shortcut returned `requested ?? true`, so a repo's only project could declare itself secondary; the partial index forbids two primaries but cannot require one, and every event carrying no PR link was then dropped. Guard moved ahead of the shortcut.

**Round 2 — three more must-fixes.**
4. **`createProject` never persisted `repoPrimary`.** It builds an explicit column whitelist and the field was not in it, so the schema default (`true`) always applied and creating a **secondary was impossible through every surface** — the row hit the unique index and the operator was told another project had "just" become primary, blaming a phantom race for what they had asked for. The unit test passed because it asserted the router's argument to a **mocked** `createProject`; only a real INSERT can see a dropped column, so the coverage is now an integration test. Eighth instance of the hand-picked-projection family, on the write path.
5. **Fixing (4) then rejected every save on a shared repo.** The SCM tab sends `repo` on every save and cannot send a role until plan 5, so updates hit the "state your role" branch — including the primary's own save, which was told to demote itself while the zero-primary rule would also refuse it, and the message named an arbitrary **secondary** as the owner. An update that does not mention the role now preserves it.
6. **The sibling lookup was not org-scoped**, so a project in another organization on the same repo counted as a sibling and its id was rendered verbatim into the operator-facing error. Pre-024 that collision was a raw 500 with no leak, so this diff introduced the disclosure.

**Round 3 — one must-fix.**
7. **`findRepoSiblingsFromDb` had no executed coverage.** The committed assertion pinned the router's *call*, not the query. Swapping its two column predicates typechecks, lints, and passes all 11152 unit tests while returning `[]` for every input — reporting "no siblings" for every duplicate save and silently disabling this plan's entire pre-check. Verbatim the argument accepted one round earlier for `createProject`, applied to the write path but not the read path. Two integration tests now catch both that swap and the dropped org predicate.

**Round 4 — converged, zero code findings.** Confirmed the six surfaces are mutually consistent, the per-request cache cannot bleed across PRs (the adapter is constructed per delivery), and `extractEventPRNumber` covers all five `PROCESSABLE_EVENTS`.

**Pattern worth recording:** five of the seven must-fixes were tests asserting a *proxy* rather than the thing — a fabricated error shape, a mocked `createProject`, a mocked sibling query, and two half-wired resolution sites. Every one passed a green suite; none was caught by the AC walk.

**Should-fixes taken:** `check_suite` sends an EMPTY `pull_requests` array when checks run on the `refs/pull/N/head` virtual ref (documented in `triggers/github/utils.ts`, which already ships the parser) — taking only `[0]` left the highest-volume event resolving no link; `repoPrimary` without `repo` now fails rather than silently writing nothing, which would have made plan 5's toggle report a success that changed nothing; removed this diff's lint warning and an orphaned docblock.

**Deferred — recorded in `docs/BUGS.md`:**
- **The worker resolves its project by repo, ignoring the PR link.** `GitHubJob` carries `repoFullName` but no `projectId`, so `worker-env.ts:47` re-resolves first-match. On a shared repo the container can get the **wrong project's credentials**, and the compensator/classifier act on the wrong lock. No plan in this spec owns `worker-env.ts`; **spec AC #8/#9 are not true end-to-end until it lands.** Fix shape: stamp the resolved `projectId` onto the job in `buildJob`.
- **Moving a project to a different repository can strand the old one** with secondaries and no primary — the zero-primary drop reached by a different door. Needs the update path to check the vacated repo.
- [x] AC #5 — `npm run typecheck` exit 0.
- [x] AC #6 — `npm test` 11152 passed / 23 skipped, exit 0; `npm run test:integration` 673 passed, exit 0.
- [x] AC #7 — `npm run lint` exit 0.

**Divergences (recorded):**
- **The plan cites one resolution site; there are two.** Besides `resolveProject`, a per-request cache feeds `isSelfAuthored` (loop prevention) and `sendReaction`. Left on first-match, a shared repo would check ONE project's bot identities against ANOTHER project's comments. Both now share one seam. Same file, so no ownership change — the task list was simply incomplete.
- **`parseWebhook` had no PR number for `check_suite`**, which nests its PRs in an array. That is the highest-volume event CASCADE routes, so link-first would have been dead on the busiest path. Stamped at parse time, since `resolveProject` receives the event and never the payload.
- **`repoPrimary` is not on `ProjectConfig`** — it exists only as a column, so a `ProjectConfig[]` sibling list cannot say which sibling is primary. Added a narrow `findRepoSiblingsFromDb` returning `{id, repoPrimary}` rather than widening the config projection. This is the **seventh** appearance of the hand-picked-projection family in this spec.
- **Four existing suites needed the new dependency stubbed**, and `projects.test.ts` asserts the exact create payload, which now carries `repoPrimary: true`. Both are the honest cost of adding a dependency to `create`, not incidental churn.
